# UST.G3FZ: H02 Component-20 Second-Jet O8 Source Theorem

## Result

The previously finite-only degree-8 Hilbert--Burch column has a selected
source description at all 48 archived split fibers.

Start with the degree-8 source space

\[
U_1=operatorname{Sym}^1D_7+operatorname{Sym}^2B_6
 +\langle\partial_iA_1\rangle+\langle\partial_iA_2\rangle.
\]

It has dimension 15. Allowing one element of this space in each of the four
rows produces five exact degree-8 syzygies, but all five lie in the
64-dimensional space inherited from the degree-1 and degree-2
Hilbert--Burch columns. Thus first-order data alone does not emit the new
column.

Now extend the source by the selected second jet

\[
U_2=U_1+operatorname{Sym}^1
 \langle\partial_i\partial_jA_0:0\leq i\leq j\leq2\rangle.
\]

The resulting degree-8 space has dimension 33. Its rowwise syzygy space has
dimension 17 and enlarges the inherited 64-plane by exactly one dimension.
Canonical reduction modulo the inherited plane gives precisely the normalized
G3FN column \(O_8\).

## Campaign

The identity is replayed independently at six roots for each of eight split
primes. At every one of the 48 fibers the profile is

```text
first-order source dimension          15
first-order syzygies                    5
new first-order quotient classes        0
second-jet source dimension             33
second-jet syzygies                     17
new second-jet quotient classes          1
```

The normalized output agrees coefficient-for-coefficient with the archived
G3FN `O8` payload at every fiber.

## Meaning

This moves the finite degree-8 column from an unexplained numerical object to
a sharply selected differential source space. The distinction is genuinely
second order: replacing the \(A_0\) second jet by the \(A_1\) or \(A_2\)
second jet does not create the new class in the anchor audit.

The result is consistent with the mapping-cone picture. First jets account
only for inherited deformations, while the selected second jet supplies the
one extension class needed to complete the four-by-three Hilbert--Burch
matrix.

